I led development on this one.

## The problem

A local government's legislative process ran on paper and in meetings: drafting a
measure, moving it through committee, holding public hearings, recording what was
decided. The process itself was well understood by the people running it. It was also
slow, almost impossible to search, and easy to lose the thread of.

## It is a state machine, not a set of forms

The temptation with government workflow software is to build forms that mirror the
paperwork. The actual requirement is a state machine. A measure moves through defined
stages, only certain roles can move it between them, and every transition needs to be
recorded with who did it and when. Once that was modelled honestly, most of the screens
became obvious.

Getting the stages right meant sitting with how the office already worked instead of
designing a tidier process and expecting people to adopt it. Software that does not
match the real procedure gets worked around, and then the official record is wrong,
which is worse than the paper it replaced.

## Audit trail and access

Legislative work keeps a paper trail for good reasons, so the digital version keeps one
too. Document versions are retained rather than overwritten, and actions are attributed.
Access is role-based, because a draft sitting in committee and a measure that has passed
are not the same document and should not have the same audience.

## Stack

Python and Django, React on the front, containerised with Docker and deployed on AWS.
In the same role I also customised Odoo Enterprise to deliver CRM, website and HRIS
modules that replaced manual processes.
